<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Xiao of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 891–886 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Xiao of Zhou was a king of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> in the early 9th century BCE, conventionally dated c. 891–886 BCE. A son of King Mu, he took the throne after his nephew King Yih, an irregular succession, and was followed by Yih's son King Yi. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> he rewarded the horse breeder Feizi with a small fief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>.</p></div>
